    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_da4c6ee8b3f3e6ceb844a2d0e890f8d5e3cc7f35b77e7ea339dc592b26ebedbc",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The sharp estimate for separated interval pairs becomes a bound for the full linear twelfth moment only through recursive scale selection. Taylor normalization first puts each separated pair into the nondegenerate curve setting; decreasing rearrangement then repeatedly separates transverse pairs, which use that bilinear estimate, from a dominant interval, which is passed to a smaller copy of the original problem until the resulting contraction closes.",
